WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.400
Let’s talk about 
this US election

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.640
what to watch

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.800
and what it all means.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.240
Starting with the
 way Americans  

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.960
pick their president. 

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.760
We’ll tell you about the battleground states 

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.360
to look out for

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.680
why the Senate race
is so important

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.120
and what could happen 
if Trump or Biden

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.840
refuse to accept defeat.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.200
Throw in a pandemic 
and you could say 

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.920
there’s never been a 
US election like it.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.480
So here’s a primer to 
help get through it all 

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.520
and prepare us for what 
might happen next.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.480
This election has been
 one big roller coaster 

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.000
with all kinds of drama 

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:53.960
right from the beginning.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.480
There’s been a lot of craziness. 

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.000
And there might still be more.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.640
So let’s take things a 
step at a time.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.320
The first thing to get 
your head around is

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.880
how Americans are going 
to be choosing

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.280
their next president.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.680
Yes, people will vote for either Donald Trump or Joe Biden

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.040
but it’s ultimately 
the Electoral College  

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.800
that decides who wins.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.360
That college is made 
  up of 538 electors. 

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.640
They’re people chosen 
from each state.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.560
The number of electors 
each state gets 

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.200
depends on how many members 
of Congress it has.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.240
So California has 55. 

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.040
But Vermont only has three. 

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.000
Now whether a state’s electors are Republican or Democrat

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.800
depends on which candidate gets the most votes 

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.000
in that state. 

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.120
So let’s take California 
as an example.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.120
Say a majority of Californians vote for Joe Biden. 

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.040
He wins the popular 
vote there.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.560
In that case all of California’s 55 electors

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.960
are going to be Democrats.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.280
It’s winner takes all no matter how small the majority.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.480
Those 55 Democrat electors  

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.280
are then obliged 
to vote for Biden.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.480
To become president 

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.720
Trump or Biden has to get at least 270 electoral votes 

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.000
that’s just over half. 

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.080
So it’s the Electoral College that matters.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.960
Not winning the popular vote. 

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.720
And 2016 is a good 
example of that. 

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.360
Donald Trump beat 
Hillary Clinton in   

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:44.520
Donald Trump beat 
Hillary Clinton in   

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.960
Michigan, Pennsylvania
 and Wisconsin 

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.040
by a total of just under 
80,000 votes.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.120
But he ended up getting all 
46 electoral votes

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.640
in those three states. 

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.520
It helped him cross the 
270 mark to win 

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.280
even though Hillary Clinton got more votes overall

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.880
nearly 3 million more.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.120
Whether the Electoral College is better or worse

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.560
than just a straightforward popular vote 

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.080
is debated basically 
every four years.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.320
But the system is written into the US Constitution.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.200
That’s hard to change.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.520
The reality today, though, is that political parties 

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.800
still focus on a 
handful of states  

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.000
just not necessarily 
the big ones.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.960
They’re known as battleground states

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.160
where the races are tight.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.800
Most states tend to vote Republican or Democrat. 

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.960
But some states, well, 
they have a history 

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.480
of swinging either way.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:58.400
Or maybe they have a diverse 
range of voters.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.040
Pennsylvania is actually
 Joe Biden’s home state.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:15.120
Voters there backed 
Barack Obama in 2012.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.400
But in 2016 it swung 
for Trump.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.920
Wisconsin’s another one.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.520
It was the big surprise of 2016.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.960
Wisconsin hadn’t voted Republican since 1984.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.000
Clinton didn’t even 
campaign there

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.920
and Trump ended up winning by just 23,000 votes.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:31.960
And there are others.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:34.200
Arizona is a question 
mark this year because  

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.960
while it’s reliably Republican

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.520
in the recent midterm elections 

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.120
a Democrat won the
Senate race there.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.600
Then there’s Florida.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.880
It’s got 29 electoral votes.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:44.880
And it’s fickle. 

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.600
But there’s a lot 
more going on 

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.440
than just the presidential contest.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:02.720
There are governor jobs

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.120
state and local offices
 up for grabs

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.520
and the congressional races.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.160
Winning the most seats in Congress is important 

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.880
because Republicans and Democrats can push through

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.000
policies they like and 
block ones they don’t.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.560
In the House of Representatives   

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.720
all the seats are up
 for re-election.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.520
Right now the Democrats have a comfortable majority. 

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.200
And they want to keep 
it that way.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.320
Same for the Republicans
 in the Senate.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.120
But their majority is slim. 

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.280
A third of seats are being contested this year.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.120
It’s why there’s been a big push by both parties     

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:35.720
to get everyone voting.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:38.960
And many Americans aren’t waiting for November 3.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.200
“Many people are starting to take advantage  of early voting and mail-in ballots.”

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.320
Because of the pandemic

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.400
more states have allowed people to post their votes.  

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.520
Turnout is expected to be big.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.200
But if you’re planning to sit down to hours of TV coverage

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.720
or stay glued to social 
media to find out  

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.000
who’s won on election night you could be waiting days

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.880
maybe weeks.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.040
Usually we get unofficial results on the night

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.560
that give us a pretty good 
idea of who’s won.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:22.200
But the long process of counting all those votes

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.520
is going to take even
 longer this year 

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.840
mainly because of so many mailed ballots.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:37.800
What happens in the meantime? 

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:39.720
That’s what some people 
are worried about.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.680
Because things could
get messy.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.600
President Trump has said 
over and over again

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.480
that those mailed-in ballots could lead to fraud 

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.360
and he might not 
accept a defeat.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.960
Several top Republicans though have played down

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.600
the idea that the president won’t concede.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.720
Trump’s press secretary 
said the same 

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:01.520
though she left 
some wiggle room.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.680
But the Democrats 
say it’s the president 

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.400
who’s trying to undermine
the election.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:30.560
If there is any dispute it could end up in the Supreme Court.

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.240
That’s what happened in 2000.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.680
That’s why the recent death of Supreme Court Justice 

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.280
Ruth Bader Ginsburg has been such a big political story.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:06.400
Ruth Bader Ginsburg has been such a big political story.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.880
She was a liberal judge. 

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:11.080
And Trump wants to replace her with a conservative one.

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:13.120
That’s important because those judges 

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:14.760
could end up being 
the kingmakers  

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.800
deciding who becomes president.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:18.720
If it comes to that.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.640
Whatever happens the president’s term officially ends

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:24.680
in 2021, January 20, at noon. 

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.120
That’s Inauguration Day.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:37.280
If there’s still no winner
 by then

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:39.200
and this would really be unprecedented   

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.080
a few things could happen. 

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.240
Some people are suggesting Nancy Pelosi

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.240
the speaker of the House of Representatives

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:46.600
is meant to take over.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:49.200
Another theory is that the House should hold a vote. 

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:53.320
That actually happened with Thomas Jefferson in the 1800s.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.400
The hope is that we 
  don’t get to that point.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.080
Trust is important.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.560
And the US has a pretty 
solid track record 

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:15.520
when it comes to free and transparent elections.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:18.080
Americans are proud 
of their democracy

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.960
even if some feel 
it’s being tested.

